1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,430
Blue gives the right text better odds five times in a row.

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:06,015
Then it has to finish two plus two.

3
00:00:06,015 --> 00:00:07,571
Blue picks five.

4
00:00:07,571 --> 00:00:09,096
So who won?

5
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:14,837
Keep that last tile in sight while we rewind.

6
00:00:14,837 --> 00:00:18,543
What does it mean to guess the next piece of text?

7
00:00:18,543 --> 00:00:22,540
Both models see exactly the same prefix.

8
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:27,172
Each divides a whole bar among possible next tokens.

9
00:00:27,172 --> 00:00:32,068
We keep the slice for the word the author actually wrote.

10
00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:36,992
It goes on the rail, and the real word slides into place.

11
00:00:36,992 --> 00:00:43,582
Even if Blue guessed wrong, its guess never becomes the next prefix.

12
00:00:43,582 --> 00:00:48,082
The two models get the same view all the way down.

13
00:00:49,633 --> 00:00:52,880
Take a tiny sentence with just two tiles.

14
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:57,666
The model gives the first correct tile half the bar.

15
00:00:57,666 --> 00:01:00,119
Once that tile is in place,

16
00:01:00,119 --> 00:01:04,040
the second gets an eighth of what survived.

17
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:07,287
Turn those cuts at right angles.

18
00:01:07,287 --> 00:01:12,507
The gold corner keeps the probability of both steps together.

19
00:01:12,507 --> 00:01:17,341
Copy the sentence and start from the same beginning.

20
00:01:17,341 --> 00:01:23,956
Each step is just as easy, but the joint probability shrinks again.

21
00:01:25,733 --> 00:01:30,936
How do we make the length of the paper stop deciding the score?

22
00:01:30,936 --> 00:01:33,902
Give both steps the same width.

23
00:01:33,902 --> 00:01:39,569
Squeeze one side, stretch the other, and keep the area fixed.

24
00:01:39,569 --> 00:01:44,182
The square's edge is the equivalent probability per token.

25
00:01:44,182 --> 00:01:50,728
Flip it: this is like choosing one tile out of four equally likely ones.

26
00:01:50,728 --> 00:01:54,199
That equivalent number is perplexity.

27
00:01:54,199 --> 00:02:02,437
Four boxes measure difficulty. The actual vocabulary can be enormous.

28
00:02:04,367 --> 00:02:08,539
For a book, the product gets too small for the computer to hold.

29
00:02:08,539 --> 00:02:11,941
Trade each probability for its surprise.

30
00:02:11,941 --> 00:02:16,645
Every time the probability halves, add one bit.

31
00:02:16,645 --> 00:02:19,998
That turns multiplication into stacking.

32
00:02:19,998 --> 00:02:23,110
Stack a bit here. Three more there.

33
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:27,091
Spread the stack equally across the two positions.

34
00:02:27,091 --> 00:02:32,784
Raise two to that average and you're back on the perplexity scale.

35
00:02:32,784 --> 00:02:38,598
A single nearly impossible token makes a very tall stack.

36
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:43,730
Now swing back to our six tiles.

37
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:48,050
Blue takes a smaller hit on each of the first five.

38
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:51,739
On the last tile its loss jumps.

39
00:02:51,739 --> 00:02:56,180
That jump can't erase the five small advantages.

40
00:02:56,180 --> 00:03:00,767
So Blue has lower perplexity on the whole sentence.

41
00:03:00,767 --> 00:03:04,504
Now look straight down the answer slot.

42
00:03:04,504 --> 00:03:07,586
Here we score what the model picks.

43
00:03:07,586 --> 00:03:13,896
Purple lands on four. The same probabilities give us two different winners.

44
00:03:15,767 --> 00:03:20,277
Hold the text still. Slide two token rulers over it.

45
00:03:20,277 --> 00:03:24,789
One sees two pieces. The other sees four.

46
00:03:24,789 --> 00:03:29,994
A per-token score now has a different unit on each side.

47
00:03:29,994 --> 00:03:36,869
Use the same tokenizer, or divide total surprise by the same raw bytes.

48
00:03:36,869 --> 00:03:39,566
The mix of material matters, too.

49
00:03:39,566 --> 00:03:44,675
Blue handles conversation better. Purple handles math better.

50
00:03:44,675 --> 00:03:48,375
Shift the mix and you can flip the winner again.

51
00:03:48,375 --> 00:03:52,792
Paloma takes those separate domain measurements seriously.

52
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,741
Suppose you're buying a system to sort customer mail.

53
00:03:57,741 --> 00:04:03,037
Purple gets more right. Blue costs less per letter.

54
00:04:03,037 --> 00:04:06,168
Slide a budget limit across the floor.

55
00:04:06,168 --> 00:04:09,463
The best score can end up outside it.

56
00:04:09,463 --> 00:04:13,770
That comparison helps you buy a system.

57
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:18,737
For a claim about the algorithm, open the machines.

58
00:04:18,737 --> 00:04:23,774
Feed both the same training data and compute budget.

59
00:04:23,774 --> 00:04:27,893
Change the machinery you actually want to test.

60
00:04:29,667 --> 00:04:32,216
Back to that gold answer tile.

61
00:04:32,216 --> 00:04:36,210
Imagine the rail carries a thousand of them.

62
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:40,998
Check each answer and you've got a test score.

63
00:04:40,998 --> 00:04:46,628
Now watch both models climb almost to the ceiling.

64
00:04:46,628 --> 00:04:49,755
Where does a better model go from there?

65
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:57,134
All right. Let's give those two models the same quiz.

66
00:04:57,134 --> 00:05:01,558
Ten little sums. Nothing fancy.

67
00:05:01,558 --> 00:05:05,085
One finishes. The other finishes.

68
00:05:05,085 --> 00:05:09,779
Both hit the ceiling. Where would a better model go?

69
00:05:09,779 --> 00:05:15,035
Now slide in a few questions they actually have to think about.

70
00:05:15,035 --> 00:05:17,707
The scores can move apart again.

71
00:05:17,707 --> 00:05:23,951
That's benchmark saturation: the test has run out of room at the top.

72
00:05:25,833 --> 00:05:28,140
Hold on to that little sum.

73
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:32,593
Suppose I have no idea and pick a tile at random.

74
00:05:32,593 --> 00:05:35,428
Only the gold one pays out.

75
00:05:35,428 --> 00:05:40,490
Now watch what happens when six wrong answers slide in.

76
00:05:40,490 --> 00:05:43,590
Same sum. Same correct tile.

77
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:49,288
The gold slice takes up less of the space I can land in.

78
00:05:49,288 --> 00:05:55,065
We've changed the odds of a lucky guess, while the arithmetic stayed put.

79
00:05:57,703 --> 00:06:02,753
M M L U spread multiple-choice questions across dozens of subjects.

80
00:06:02,753 --> 00:06:08,697
Its Pro version made the choice sets bigger and the questions harder.

81
00:06:08,697 --> 00:06:12,439
It cleaned up ambiguous questions, too.

82
00:06:12,439 --> 00:06:18,176
The authors compared answering directly with working through the problem first.

83
00:06:18,176 --> 00:06:22,721
Working it through helped more on the new test.

84
00:06:22,721 --> 00:06:30,524
G P Q A brought in subject experts to write questions a quick search couldn't settle.

85
00:06:30,524 --> 00:06:35,482
Humanity's Last Exam screened submissions against strong models.

86
00:06:35,482 --> 00:06:40,315
Some of the questions that survived even need a picture.

87
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:47,317
Now try a job from my phone. Help me cancel today's appointment.

88
00:06:47,317 --> 00:06:51,011
Both replies get the cancellation across.

89
00:06:51,011 --> 00:06:54,295
One leaves room to pick another day.

90
00:06:54,295 --> 00:07:00,042
I might like that. You might just want to get on with your day.

91
00:07:00,042 --> 00:07:05,456
So hide the names and ask which reply people would actually use.

92
00:07:05,456 --> 00:07:09,997
People can pick either one, or call it a tie.

93
00:07:09,997 --> 00:07:13,273
That's the idea behind Chatbot Arena.

94
00:07:14,833 --> 00:07:17,274
Each vote connects just two models.

95
00:07:17,274 --> 00:07:24,918
Blue beats purple. Purple beats gray. Gray can still beat blue.

96
00:07:24,918 --> 00:07:27,495
We keep all three results.

97
00:07:27,495 --> 00:07:33,771
Fit scores so their gaps predict the pattern of wins as well as possible.

98
00:07:33,771 --> 00:07:41,012
Close scores mean a tight match, for this crowd and these questions.

99
00:07:41,012 --> 00:07:46,212
Waiting for people is slow when you're testing changes all day.

100
00:07:46,212 --> 00:07:51,923
So give a model the judge's seat. How does it decide?

101
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:58,112
Give it a job: check that the cancellation is clear.

102
00:07:58,112 --> 00:08:02,506
And check for any promise the reply just made up.

103
00:08:02,506 --> 00:08:05,892
The judge picks the first reply on the left.

104
00:08:05,892 --> 00:08:10,958
Watch the two slips. Keep every word; swap the order.

105
00:08:10,958 --> 00:08:13,130
It picks left again.

106
00:08:13,130 --> 00:08:17,627
That's a different reply! The vote followed the seat.

107
00:08:17,627 --> 00:08:21,710
Judge both orders and keep track of the flips.

108
00:08:23,533 --> 00:08:26,132
Leave the slips where they are now.

109
00:08:26,132 --> 00:08:31,275
Pad the reply on the right. Just repeat what it already says.

110
00:08:31,275 --> 00:08:34,030
And the vote swings over.

111
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:38,149
Train against that score and the model has a shortcut.

112
00:08:38,149 --> 00:08:42,714
Say the same thing, then say it again.

113
00:08:42,714 --> 00:08:44,735
There's your length bias.

114
00:08:44,735 --> 00:08:51,214
What if we cut every answer down to the same size? Hold that thought.

115
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:58,304
Keep the answers intact. Put length difference along the floor.

116
00:08:58,304 --> 00:09:02,627
Now lift the estimated win rate above it.

117
00:09:02,627 --> 00:09:06,597
Alpaca Eval learns the pattern from many comparisons.

118
00:09:06,597 --> 00:09:12,100
It accounts for the model and the question, as well as the length.

119
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:18,710
Walk back to zero. What win rate does the fit predict at equal length?

120
00:09:18,710 --> 00:09:21,700
Average those predictions across questions.

121
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:28,008
If the extra line gives me a new appointment time, people might want it.

122
00:09:28,008 --> 00:09:31,357
So check that kind of change with people, too.

123
00:09:33,233 --> 00:09:37,189
By the way, I'm about to close that chat.

124
00:09:37,189 --> 00:09:41,028
The cancellation message reads beautifully.

125
00:09:41,028 --> 00:09:45,974
Look behind it. The appointment is still in my calendar.

126
00:09:45,974 --> 00:09:48,848
We judged what the model wrote.

127
00:09:48,848 --> 00:09:52,829
Now I want to know what it can get done.

128
00:09:52,829 --> 00:09:59,203
Give it the tools, stand back, and check what actually changed.

129
00:10:01,067 --> 00:10:05,218
Would you let either model touch your checkout?

130
00:10:05,218 --> 00:10:09,862
This shop offers free shipping from a hundred dollars up.

131
00:10:09,862 --> 00:10:13,530
The orders on either side look fine.

132
00:10:13,530 --> 00:10:18,135
Right on the cutoff, the charge sticks.

133
00:10:18,135 --> 00:10:22,421
Blue gives a lovely explanation.

134
00:10:22,421 --> 00:10:26,716
Purple opens the code and changes one sign.

135
00:10:26,716 --> 00:10:32,378
Which one has actually saved the customer anything?

136
00:10:34,133 --> 00:10:38,704
The patch goes into the version of the project that had the bug.

137
00:10:38,704 --> 00:10:43,530
Here's a faster patch: make all shipping free.

138
00:10:43,530 --> 00:10:49,182
That clears the complaint. It also breaks a perfectly good order.

139
00:10:49,182 --> 00:10:51,021
So check both:

140
00:10:51,021 --> 00:10:53,755
does the failing case pass now,

141
00:10:53,755 --> 00:10:57,960
and do the old passing cases still pass?

142
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:01,775
Changing the boundary keeps the other orders intact.

143
00:11:01,775 --> 00:11:10,404
SWE-bench uses real project issues and executable tests to judge the patch.

144
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:16,413
Keep the model. Change what happens around it.

145
00:11:16,413 --> 00:11:20,291
On the left it submits once and walks away.

146
00:11:20,291 --> 00:11:24,501
On the right it opens a file, runs a test,

147
00:11:24,501 --> 00:11:28,634
and gets the failing result back before it submits.

148
00:11:28,634 --> 00:11:32,513
That loop lives in the scaffold around the model.

149
00:11:32,513 --> 00:11:37,437
The score can go up even when the weights stay put.

150
00:11:37,437 --> 00:11:40,219
Lock the loop to compare models.

151
00:11:40,219 --> 00:11:43,331
Lock the model to compare loops.

152
00:11:43,331 --> 00:11:46,980
Give both runs the same budget.

153
00:11:47,633 --> 00:11:52,637
Now start the same job four times from scratch.

154
00:11:52,637 --> 00:11:57,763
Here are four separate patches. Only one passes.

155
00:11:57,763 --> 00:12:02,182
If you take one at random, the odds are easy.

156
00:12:02,182 --> 00:12:06,698
What if you take two, without putting the first one back?

157
00:12:06,698 --> 00:12:11,824
Before you answer, look for the pairs around the good patch.

158
00:12:13,726 --> 00:12:16,885
The good patch has three possible partners.

159
00:12:16,885 --> 00:12:20,492
The three remaining pairs all miss it.

160
00:12:20,492 --> 00:12:26,592
One try counts individual patches. Two tries count pairs.

161
00:12:26,592 --> 00:12:29,351
That's the idea of pass at k:

162
00:12:29,351 --> 00:12:33,926
in k attempts, how often does at least one work?

163
00:12:33,926 --> 00:12:38,235
In a real run, sample several times per problem,

164
00:12:38,235 --> 00:12:43,487
estimate from the combinations, then average across problems.

165
00:12:43,487 --> 00:12:47,675
Finding a good patch somewhere in the pile is one thing.

166
00:12:47,675 --> 00:12:51,209
Picking it as your one submission is another.

167
00:12:52,933 --> 00:12:57,936
These square pieces can ask a very different question.

168
00:12:57,936 --> 00:13:01,910
Pick an input and face it toward its output.

169
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:06,340
From the side you can see they're two different grids.

170
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:10,817
The gray shape moved right; gold stayed put.

171
00:13:10,817 --> 00:13:15,653
The next example tempts us to say, always move two.

172
00:13:15,653 --> 00:13:19,149
Try that on the third. Something's off.

173
00:13:19,149 --> 00:13:23,195
The distance changed, but the meeting point didn't.

174
00:13:23,195 --> 00:13:26,906
Slide right until the two shapes touch.

175
00:13:27,533 --> 00:13:31,272
Now here's an input whose output is hidden.

176
00:13:31,272 --> 00:13:33,886
Keep the gray shape intact.

177
00:13:33,886 --> 00:13:35,769
Where should it stop?

178
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:40,441
Right there. One cell does it.

179
00:13:40,441 --> 00:13:46,491
That's an ARC-AGI puzzle: learn a transformation from a few examples,

180
00:13:46,491 --> 00:13:51,414
then apply it to a new grid. Every cell has to match.

181
00:13:51,414 --> 00:13:57,121
A system can try several rules and replay each on the examples.

182
00:13:57,121 --> 00:14:00,281
That's compute spent at test time.

183
00:14:00,281 --> 00:14:06,159
If two rules fit, one more useful example can separate them.

184
00:14:06,159 --> 00:14:10,886
A bigger search budget can't supply that missing evidence.

185
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:18,184
One last job. Our model answers messages for a theater.

186
00:14:18,184 --> 00:14:20,373
Tell me when the show starts.

187
00:14:20,373 --> 00:14:23,200
Send me the unpublished script.

188
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:28,011
Blocking both keeps the script in, and keeps the audience out.

189
00:14:28,011 --> 00:14:32,753
HarmBench checks whether a reply enables a specified harmful behavior.

190
00:14:32,753 --> 00:14:38,887
It judges what the reply does; our playhouse checks access.

191
00:14:38,887 --> 00:14:42,580
Test ordinary requests separately, too.

192
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:48,326
And when every light turns green, ask one last question:

193
00:14:48,326 --> 00:14:53,249
is this the first time the system has seen the test?

194
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,773
Take that passing test and turn it over.

195
00:14:58,773 --> 00:15:02,665
There is an old copy tucked behind it.

196
00:15:02,665 --> 00:15:09,105
Same question. Same answer, right down to the last symbol.

197
00:15:09,105 --> 00:15:12,738
Suppose Blue saw that copy in training.

198
00:15:12,738 --> 00:15:16,866
Our green check still tells us what it answered.

199
00:15:16,866 --> 00:15:20,145
The interesting question is what happens

200
00:15:20,145 --> 00:15:22,433
when we pull the copy away.

201
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:26,173
Go back to the shipping rule.

202
00:15:26,173 --> 00:15:31,179
At a hundred dollars, memorizing zero buys you the point.

203
00:15:31,179 --> 00:15:34,669
Now slide the threshold to one twenty.

204
00:15:34,669 --> 00:15:37,148
Try an order for one nineteen.

205
00:15:37,148 --> 00:15:40,398
The old shortcut still says free.

206
00:15:40,398 --> 00:15:44,249
The new rule says eight dollars.

207
00:15:44,249 --> 00:15:49,785
When test material gets into training, we call that contamination.

208
00:15:49,785 --> 00:15:55,069
A fresh case gives those two explanations a chance to disagree.

209
00:15:56,733 --> 00:16:01,766
With the training data in hand, we can go looking for a copy.

210
00:16:01,766 --> 00:16:06,002
Put a little window over three neighboring tokens.

211
00:16:06,002 --> 00:16:08,729
Slide it along the test item.

212
00:16:08,729 --> 00:16:12,118
Look for those exact runs in the corpus.

213
00:16:12,118 --> 00:16:16,355
The lecture's thirteen-token window works the same way.

214
00:16:16,355 --> 00:16:20,035
A shared heading can light up by accident.

215
00:16:20,035 --> 00:16:23,503
A paraphrase can slip right through.

216
00:16:23,503 --> 00:16:28,682
The match tells you where to look; you decide what to remove.

217
00:16:30,367 --> 00:16:34,903
What if all we can see is the model's output probabilities?

218
00:16:34,903 --> 00:16:38,659
Take three independent test questions.

219
00:16:38,659 --> 00:16:42,927
Keep each answer stuck to its own question.

220
00:16:42,927 --> 00:16:47,757
Now shuffle the cards, not the answers.

221
00:16:47,757 --> 00:16:52,391
Give the model the whole stack as a sequence of text.

222
00:16:52,391 --> 00:16:57,098
We can score how much probability it puts on that sequence.

223
00:16:57,098 --> 00:16:59,903
Do the same for the published order.

224
00:17:01,467 --> 00:17:04,613
Add more cards and score a bunch of shuffles.

225
00:17:04,613 --> 00:17:07,477
Most land in this little cluster.

226
00:17:07,477 --> 00:17:12,873
The published order is the gold dot. Why is it way out there?

227
00:17:12,873 --> 00:17:17,732
A model that remembers the stack has a reason to like it.

228
00:17:17,732 --> 00:17:24,049
But these questions have to be exchangeable under the fresh-data assumption.

229
00:17:24,049 --> 00:17:30,953
Put dates on the cards, and chronological order gets an advantage for free.

230
00:17:30,953 --> 00:17:35,812
Now the shuffle is answering a different question.

231
00:17:37,633 --> 00:17:43,198
You can save yourself some detective work by designing for freshness up front.

232
00:17:43,198 --> 00:17:47,226
Freeze the model version and mark its training cutoff.

233
00:17:47,226 --> 00:17:51,336
Put the test on the far side of that line.

234
00:17:51,336 --> 00:17:56,953
LiveCodeBench dates its programming problems, so you can pick a later window.

235
00:17:56,953 --> 00:18:01,748
Keep another set out of public circulation for a final check.

236
00:18:01,748 --> 00:18:08,187
On the next run, write down which model and which test you actually used.

237
00:18:08,933 --> 00:18:12,278
A fresh exam can still be the wrong exam.

238
00:18:12,278 --> 00:18:16,900
My shop needs a shipping spreadsheet someone can use tomorrow.

239
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:23,207
There are coupons, partial refunds, and files other people have to open.

240
00:18:23,207 --> 00:18:30,428
That distance between the test and the real job is what ecological validity is about.

241
00:18:30,428 --> 00:18:34,496
GDPval starts from professional deliverables.

242
00:18:34,496 --> 00:18:40,032
MedHELM separates jobs like drafting a clinical note and supporting a decision.

243
00:18:40,032 --> 00:18:45,193
A score goes further when its task looks like yours.

244
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:54,683
So give both systems the same tools, time limit, and unseen orders.

245
00:18:54,683 --> 00:18:58,554
Set aside the orders right on the boundary.

246
00:18:58,554 --> 00:19:05,699
Then add a nasty one: refund half of a one-twenty order.

247
00:19:05,699 --> 00:19:10,362
Write the rule before either model gets to work.

248
00:19:10,362 --> 00:19:13,164
Recompute shipping on the amount left.

249
